<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The tile the notebook sits on: assets/icon_mk7.svg's rounded rect, minus the rounding, which the
  launcher's own mask supplies.

  mk7 changed nothing but this. The notebook, the disc and the ribbon are mk6's to the coordinate;
  the tile went from a flat #0C0B0B to a vertical gradient, #463E3F at the top to #363031 at the
  bottom — which are the app's own dark `surfaceVariant` and page, so the icon is now the dark theme's
  colours and not a colour of its own. A drawable rather than a colour resource because a colour
  cannot hold a gradient; the adaptive icon takes either.

  THE GRADIENT SPANS THE VISIBLE 72dp, NOT THE 108dp LAYER. The launcher shows only the centre 72dp
  of the layer and the outer 18dp is bleed, so mk7's top and bottom stops — drawn across its 779-unit
  canvas, which is that visible tile — land at y=18 and y=90 here. Spanning 0..108 instead would
  show only the middle two thirds of the ramp. The bleed is clamped to the end colours, which is what
  a parallax shift reveals.

  The old tile's notes on contrast are moot for mk6 onwards: the disc under the mark is what every
  dark edge is now dark against, and the tile only has to be a tile. Its remaining job is the one
  #000000 failed at — having an edge of its own against a black wallpaper — and #363031 at the bottom
  keeps that.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:aapt="http://schemas.android.com/aapt"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">
    <path android:pathData="M0,0h108v108h-108z">
        <aapt:attr name="android:fillColor">
            <gradient
                android:type="linear"
                android:startX="54"
                android:startY="18"
                android:endX="54"
                android:endY="90">
                <item android:offset="0" android:color="#FF463E3F" />
                <item android:offset="1" android:color="#FF363031" />
            </gradient>
        </aapt:attr>
    </path>
</vector>
